utral":0.5862,"def_epa_per_play":0.1565},{"week":17,"opponent":"KC","home":false,"pf":21.0,"pa":31.0,"off_snaps":74,"epa_per_play":0.1292,"pass_rate":0.6622,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6512,"def_epa_per_play":0.2047}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2025,"team":"DAL","distance":0.5183,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Brian Schottenheimer"},{"season":2022,"team":"BUF","distance":0.5423,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2020,"team":"WAS","distance":0.5748,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2019,"team":"CHI","distance":0.6142,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Matt Nagy"},{"season":2025,"team":"DEN","distance":0.6246,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Sean Payton"}],"defense":[{"season":2021,"team":"LV","distance":0.6405,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2018,"team":"SF","distance":0.9108,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Robert Saleh"},{"season":2022,"team":"LA","distance":0.9701,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Raheem Morris"},{"season":2019,"team":"DAL","distance":0.9919,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"GB","distance":0.9941,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Chargers went 5-11 in 2019 and were outscored 345 to 337. The offense carried the team, ranking 11th in EPA per play while the defense finished 21st in EPA allowed. What set them apart was the lowest 12 personnel usage and the highest Cover 3 rate in the league.","offense":"The Chargers offense was a little above average by EPA per play. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the lowest 12 personnel usage in the league. Philip Rivers ran the passing game and leaned heavily on Keenan Allen, who drew 26% of the targets.","defense":"The Chargers defense was a little below average by EPA allowed per play. Its strongest area was explosive play rate allowed, where it ranked 8th, and its weakest was takeaways per game, where it ranked 31st. Melvin Ingram led the way with 7 sacks. In terms of scheme, they had the highest Cover 3 rate in the league.","special":"The Chargers were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 76% of field goals, which was well below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into receivers, about 17% of the money on the books. Their quarterbacks were among the oldest in the league.","players":"Philip Rivers played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 4,615 yards. Keenan Allen was the clear top target with 26% of the looks and 1,199 receiving yards. The backfield was a committee led by Melvin Gordon and Austin Ekeler. On defense, Joey Bosa led the team with 11.5 sacks and Rayshawn Jenkins picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"Shane Steichen called the offense, and the defensive play caller for this season is not in the coaching data. Their offense looked most like the 2025 Cowboys under Brian Schottenheimer and the 2022 Bills. Their defense looked most like the 2021 Raiders and the 2018 49ers under Robert Saleh.","trends":"The offense went from 4th in EPA per play in 2018 to 11th in 2019, a modest decline. The defense moved from 10th in EPA allowed per play in 2018 to 21st in 2019, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was two point attempt rate, which went from 16% to 3%.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2019. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}